From eb642f0e9df46ec99e7c7c94db77bc27dd9eff26 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: brunduk <nikolai.viktorovich@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:59:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] d/wmr: turntable refit of the left gyro matrix -- roll
 machine-pinned; twist DOF honestly unresolved

The turntable audit convicted 0068: its roll handling was 154.8deg off the
machine-measured target (hand-capture noise near worst case). Refit: roll residual
0/7.2deg (fwd/rev held-out). Structural limit: one turntable axis leaves the twist
DOF about that axis undetermined; the only twist sources (hand pitch/yaw phases)
disagree by 140.6deg at 46-58deg internal noise each -- phi_pitch chosen as less-bad,
documented unresolved. Final: pitch 11.6deg, yaw 133deg ambiguity. THE definitive
close needs one more turntable orientation (the 3-axis cradle box, next session):
a second machine axis fully determines the matrix with zero human motion.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c | 118 ++++++++++++++++--------
 1 file changed, 77 insertions(+), 41 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c
index 1397f8fc1..3f6477303 100644
--- a/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c
+++ b/src/xrt/drivers/wmr/wmr_controller_hp.c
@@ -37,20 +37,25 @@
 // bad axis enters the fusion filter, before it gets integrated. Default off; LEFT hand only.
 DEBUG_GET_ONCE_BOOL_OPTION(wmr_controller_left_yaw_gyro_invert, "WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_YAW_GYRO_INVERT", false)
 
-// WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_GYRO_FIT (2026-08-17, T208 followup): WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_YAW_GYRO_INVERT
-// above corrects the dominant sign flip on the left controller's yaw axis, but the wearer's own
-// figure-8 test (T208) found a residual: the left gizmo "sigue acumulando algo y rotando de a
-// poco drifteando sobre un eje fantasma medio combinado" -- a small cross-axis misalignment that
-// a pure per-axis sign flip cannot remove, because non-commutative integration of even a 1-3
-// degree axis tilt winds up over a multi-turn motion instead of cancelling. This flag replaces
-// the sign-only correction with the full fitted left-to-right rotation matrix below (applied
-// AFTER the Y-invert above, on top of it, not instead of it -- see the gating assert at the use
-// site). Fitted from a labeled RIGHT-then-LEFT capture (WMR_CONTROLLER_CALIBRATION_LOG=1; each
-// hand: still 10s / 8x pure pitch / still / 8x pure roll / still / 8x pure yaw / still) taken
-// WITH WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_YAW_GYRO_INVERT=1 already active, so this matrix is relative to the
-// already-sign-fixed left stream, not the raw one. Default off, for a live wearer A/B. Gyro only
-// -- WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_YAW_GYRO_INVERT's own comment above already measured (T207) that the
-// analogous accel extension made things worse (precession), so this stays gyro-only too.
+// WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_GYRO_FIT (2026-08-17, T209 hybrid turntable+hand refit):
+// WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_YAW_GYRO_INVERT above corrects the dominant sign flip on the left
+// controller's yaw axis, but the wearer's own figure-8 test (T208) found a residual: the left
+// gizmo "sigue acumulando algo y rotando de a poco drifteando sobre un eje fantasma medio
+// combinado" -- a small cross-axis misalignment that a pure per-axis sign flip cannot remove,
+// because non-commutative integration of even a small axis tilt winds up over a multi-turn
+// motion instead of cancelling. This flag replaces the sign-only correction with the full
+// fitted left-to-right rotation matrix below (applied AFTER the Y-invert above, on top of it,
+// not instead of it -- see the gating assert at the use site). The matrix baked in below was
+// refit 2026-08-17 (T209) from a machine-grade turntable capture (roll axis, both spin
+// directions, both hands -- see the long comment at the use site for the numbers), which
+// found the original T208 hand-only fit's roll handling was close to 154.8 deg wrong, not
+// merely noisy; the turntable data replaces T208's roll input outright. The twist about that
+// roll axis is still only constrained by the same noisy 8-rep hand captures T208 used (pitch
+// and yaw phases), and those disagree with each other by 140.6 deg on what that twist should
+// be -- so treat the roll-axis correction here as solid and the remaining twist as a live
+// wearer A/B question, same as before. Default off. Gyro only -- WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_YAW_GYRO_
+// INVERT's own comment above already measured (T207) that the analogous accel extension made
+// things worse (precession), so this stays gyro-only too.
 DEBUG_GET_ONCE_BOOL_OPTION(wmr_controller_left_gyro_fit, "WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_GYRO_FIT", false)
 
 #define WMR_TRACE(ctrl, ...) U_LOG_XDEV_IFL_T(&ctrl->base.base, ctrl->base.log_level, __VA_ARGS__)
@@ -432,37 +437,68 @@ wmr_controller_hp_packet_parse(struct wmr_controller_hp *ctrl, const unsigned ch
 		// bien"), leaving only a constant heading offset -- which is the separate
 		// no-absolute-yaw-reference problem, not a frame error.
 
-		// WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_GYRO_FIT (2026-08-17, T208 followup): the exact left-to-right
-		// fitted matrix, composed on top of the Y-invert above (FIT requires INVERT=1, as
-		// captured -- it is meaningless applied to the raw un-inverted stream, hence nested
-		// inside this same `if`). Derivation: per-hand, per-phase (pitch/roll/yaw) dominant
-		// rotation axis was estimated from WMR_CONTROLLER_CALIBRATION_LOG=1 samples with
-		// |gyro| > 1 rad/s, sign-aligned to a running mean, averaged and normalized to a
-		// unit vector; M0 = A_right @ inv(A_left) over the 3 phase pairs, then orthogonalized
-		// via SVD (M = U @ Vt, det(M) landed at +1.0 without forcing it -- consistent with
-		// this being a small residual on top of an already sign-corrected stream, not a
-		// second reflection). Fitted 2026-08-17 (T208 followup capture, ~vr/jack-in-
-		// wayland.log). Per-phase residual angle between M @ a_left and a_right, RIGHT
-		// controller's own axis as ground truth: pitch 6.3 deg, roll 9.7 deg, yaw 11.0 deg
-		// (down from 6.8/31.4/11.8 deg raw, before this fit). NOTE, measured honestly: those
-		// residuals, and M's own 21.8 deg axis-angle deviation from identity, are much larger
-		// than the "1-3 degree" cross-axis residual the figure-8 test's own qualitative
-		// description suggested -- the roll phase alone contributed a 31 deg raw mismatch
-		// between hands. This is a single 8-rep capture per axis with no repeat trials, and
-		// both hands independently show real, systematic off-axis leakage during the roll
-		// phase (confirmed via PCA on the raw samples, ~90%+ variance explained by one axis,
-		// i.e. not sensor noise) -- some of what M is fitting here may be this session's own
-		// left/right hand biomechanical asymmetry rather than a fixed hardware property of
-		// the controller. Do not treat this as a settled correction the way the Y-invert is;
-		// it needs the wearer's live A/B, precisely because the numbers here are bigger than
-		// expected.
+		// WMR_CONTROLLER_LEFT_GYRO_FIT (2026-08-17, T209 hybrid turntable+hand refit,
+		// REPLACES the T208 hand-only fit below): composed on top of the Y-invert above,
+		// same nesting/gating as before (meaningless without INVERT=1). The T208 fit was
+		// built entirely from noisy 8-rep hand captures; a same-night TURNTABLE session
+		// (machine-grade, constant-omega, both hands mounted face-down in turn, both spin
+		// directions captured on both hands) gave a ground-truth measurement of the ROLL
+		// axis correspondence that the hand-only fit could never match, and the result is a
+		// real correction, not a tweak: the T208 matrix, evaluated on the true pre-fit
+		// turntable input, points its roll axis 154.8 deg away from what the RIGHT
+		// controller reports for the physically identical spin (barely better than the RAW,
+		// uncorrected mismatch of 175.2/177.7 deg measured on both spin directions) -- i.e.
+		// T208's roll handling was not "off by a bit", it was close to the worst it could
+		// have been. Both spin directions on both hands agree the raw relationship is a
+		// near-total sign flip (~176-178 deg, not the ~155 deg T208 left in place), which is
+		// exactly what mirrored controller shells sharing one right-hand-rule convention per
+		// hand predict for the SAME physical rotation (see the T209 session note for the
+		// physical argument) -- matching conventions requires the map to send LEFT's
+		// measured roll axis close to -1x itself onto RIGHT's, i.e. effectively Z -> -Z, not
+		// Z -> Z.
+		//
+		// Derivation: turntable slow-forward gives one very clean axis pair (RIGHT
+		// (+0.0345,+0.0001,+0.9994), 360 s, 2.5 deg internal scatter; LEFT pre-fit
+		// (+0.0066,+0.0720,-0.9974), inferred by applying the OLD T208 matrix's inverse to
+		// the logged post-fit turntable samples). The minimal rotation R_base solving
+		// R_base @ a_left = b_right for that single pair (175.2 deg about
+		// (+0.869,-0.494,-0.030)) is checked, not assumed, against the independently
+		// captured REVERSE spin on both hands: R_base's own reverse-pair residual is 8.6 deg
+		// -- good self-consistency for a completely held-out data point. A turntable can only
+		// ever probe ONE physical axis, so this pins 2 of the 3 rotational degrees of freedom
+		// (where the roll axis goes) and leaves exactly one -- the twist about that axis --
+		// undetermined by turntable data alone, no matter how many times it's re-spun. The
+		// same 8-rep hand-capture pitch/yaw phases used by T208 are the only source of
+		// information on that remaining twist, brought in at low weight given their quality:
+		// re-measured tonight, they show 46-58 deg of INTERNAL angular scatter (angle of each
+		// raw sample from its own segment's mean axis) -- far above the turntable's 2.5-3.5
+		// deg, i.e. at the hand-capture noise floor T208 already flagged. Solving for the
+		// twist angle that best matches each phase independently gives phi_pitch = +29.0 deg
+		// (residual 11.6 deg) and phi_yaw = -111.6 deg (residual 31.7 deg) -- a 140.6 deg
+		// DISAGREEMENT between the two, which is the honest headline of this fit: the twist
+		// component is not reliably determined by tonight's data. phi_pitch is used below
+		// (lower residual, and it also mildly improves the held-out reverse-spin check, 8.6
+		// -> 7.2 deg) but this is "less bad of two noisy signals", not a resolved number --
+		// the roll-axis correction is the trustworthy part of this fit, the twist is not.
+		//
+		// Full residuals of the shipped matrix M against all four measured pairs: slow-fwd
+		// 0.0 deg (fit point), slow-rev 7.2 deg, pitch 11.6 deg, yaw 133.5 deg (yaw was NOT
+		// used to set the twist and disagrees badly with it, consistent with the phi
+		// disagreement above -- do not read this as the fit being wrong on yaw specifically,
+		// read it as "twist is unresolved"). det(M) = 1.0 (proper rotation, unforced), angle
+		// from identity 175.4 deg. Data: ~vr/jack-in-wayland.log, T209 turntable session
+		// (2026-08-17 22:xx-23:01), cross-checked against the T208 hand-capture segments
+		// earlier in the same log. Still default off, for a live wearer A/B -- if the
+		// figure-8 ghost-axis residual clears, the roll fix is doing its job regardless of
+		// how well the twist landed; if orientation looks wrong in a NEW way (rather than
+		// just "still drifting"), suspect the twist first.
 		if (debug_get_bool_option_wmr_controller_left_gyro_fit()) {
 			static const struct xrt_matrix_3x3 left_gyro_fit = {
 			    .v =
 			        {
-			            0.992832054f,  0.017066473f,  0.118293056f,
-			            0.025801456f,  0.935808594f,  -0.351563024f,
-			            -0.116699600f, 0.352095172f,  0.928660429f,
+			            0.863502032f,  -0.500137398f, -0.065014026f,
+			            -0.503813269f, -0.861313889f, -0.065654970f,
+			            -0.023160977f, 0.089448129f,  -0.995722151f,
 			        },
 			};
 			math_matrix_3x3_transform_vec3(&left_gyro_fit, &last_input->imu.gyro, &last_input->imu.gyro);
-- 
2.47.3

